      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom are turning all yellow and kind of pale. any idea what happened or how to fix it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yellowing leaves on tomato plants can be a bit of a mystery, but I'm sure it's not a case of goblins stealing all the nutrients from the soil. I've heard that in some cases, it's due to a lack of calcium, which can cause the leaves to turn yellow. However, it's also possible that it's just a sign of nutrient deficiencies or overwatering.\n\nYou might want to check the soil pH and make sure it's not too acidic or alkaline, as that can affect nutrient availability. It's also a good idea to inspect the roots for any signs of damage or disease. I've seen some gardeners use a little trick to diagnose root problems: they'll gently remove a leaf from the plant and examine the underside for any signs of pests or disease. If you find any, you can treat the plant with some insecticidal soap or neem oil.\n\nOne thing that might be worth trying is to prune the lower leaves to encourage new growth from the top of the plant. This is a bit like how a goblin might clear out a cluttered cave to make way for new treasures – it's all about creating space for new growth! Just be sure to disinfect your pruning tools to avoid",
