      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, I just can't help but think of goblins when it comes to soil pH – it's like they have a special fondness for acidic environments, don't you think? Anyway, back to your tomatoes!\n\nTo check the soil pH, you don't necessarily need a special kit, but a pH test strip or a DIY method using baking soda and vinegar can give you a good idea. You can purchase pH test strips at most gardening stores or online. These strips change color depending on the pH level of the soil, so it's a quick and easy way to get an idea of where you stand.\n\nAlternatively, you can mix 1 tablespoon of baking soda with 1 tablespoon of water to create a pH indicator solution. If the solution turns pink, your soil is likely alkaline (high pH). If it turns blue, your soil is likely acidic (low pH). And if it turns green, your soil is probably just right (neutral pH).\n\nNow, if you do find that your soil pH is the problem, you can adjust it by adding lime to raise the pH or sulfur to lower it. Just be careful not to overdo it, or you might end up with a soil pH that's as unpredictable as a g",
