    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adding lime to acidic soil is a bit like trying to tame a mischievous goblin – you need to be careful not to overdo it. Too much lime can burn the roots of your tomato plant, while too little might not make a significant difference.\n\nThe amount of lime you need will depend on the severity of the acidity and the type of soil you have. A general rule of thumb is to start with a small amount, such as 1-2 cups of dolomitic lime per 100 square feet of garden bed. You can mix it into the top 6-8 inches of soil, then water it in thoroughly.\n\nIt's also a good idea to test the soil pH again after adding the lime to make sure it's not too high. You can use a pH test strip or send a sample to a lab for a more accurate reading.\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking – what if I add too much lime? Well, it's like trying to outsmart a goblin: it's not always easy. If you add too much lime, you might see signs of nutrient deficiencies, such as yellowing leaves or stunted growth. In extreme cases, you might even see a buildup of calcium in the soil",
